<Shell title="The angle behind the design" review>
<section class="frame page-heading"><span class="micro">Website study 02 / Research & recommendation / 07 Sep 2026</span><h1>Start with the setup<br />they can afford.</h1><p>The owner already has equipment from different makers, bought over time. Origin89’s opportunity is to help them understand and manage that setup, with a path to add more when it makes sense.</p></section>
<section class="frame page-body">
<div class="two-column"><div><span class="micro">Recommended headline</span><h2>Make your equipment<br />work together.</h2></div><div><p style="font-size:21px">You built your cottage setup one piece at a time. Origin89 is being built to connect solar, batteries, backup power and sensors from different makers, so you can manage them in one place.</p><div class="actions"><a class="action" href="/next/equipment-first/">Review direction A ↗</a></div></div></div>
<section class="subsection"><span class="micro">Why this angle</span><h2>Compatibility has a human reason.</h2><p>The founder’s observation is that neighbours have mixed equipment because a complete replacement or a single-brand system can be out of reach. Treat that as a strong interview hypothesis, not market-wide survey data or a verified price comparison.</p><ol class="plain-list"><li><strong>Lead with the existing investment.</strong> Keep useful equipment and make a site built in stages easier to manage.</li><li><strong>Prove the connection path.</strong> Let people search exact models and see what is researched, implemented or validated.</li><li><strong>Show one useful outcome.</strong> A sample battery and temperature view makes the first step easier to understand.</li><li><strong>Explain local control.</strong> State what runs at the site and what needs a remote connection.</li><li><strong>Back ownership with an open stack.</strong> Hardware, firmware, apps and documented interfaces need accessible source and licence pages.</li></ol></section>
<section class="subsection"><span class="micro">Competitors & references</span><h2>What to learn.<br />Where to be specific.</h2><p>These are official-source observations, followed by our interpretation for Origin89. They are not a product test or a claim that every alternative serves the same installation.</p><div class="research-grid">{references.map(r=><article><span class="micro">{r.category}</span><h3>{r.name}</h3><p>{r.observation}</p><p style="margin-top:16px"><strong>For Origin89:</strong> {r.lesson}</p><a href={r.url}>Official source ↗</a></article>)}</div></section>
<section class="subsection"><span class="micro">Beyond the brochure</span><h2>A website that helps<br />before it sells.</h2><div class="research-grid"><article><span class="micro">01 / Discovery</span><h3>Find my equipment.</h3><p>Make the catalogue a useful place to check a model, understand the evidence and ask an exact question. Publish substantive model pages when their status is ready; avoid thousands of thin compatibility claims.</p><a href="/next/equipment/">Try the catalogue ↗</a></article><article><span class="micro">02 / First useful result</span><h3>Show me a small setup.</h3><p>Let an owner explore a battery, solar and temperature view before learning the entire product family. A modest starting point fits the way cottage systems grow.</p><a href="/next/sample-site/">Try the sample site ↗</a></article><article><span class="micro">03 / Qualification</span><h3>Build around what I have.</h3><p>Carry an equipment model into an enquiry about one concrete job. This could produce clearer integration demand and more useful conversations than a generic mailing-list signup.</p><a href="/next/plan/">Try a site enquiry ↗</a></article></div></section>
<section class="subsection"><span class="micro">Business hypotheses to test</span><h2>Sell help getting it working.</h2><p>Open source can support a business around a ready-to-use Controller, commissioning and ongoing support. Those are options to evaluate, not offers or pricing decided in this draft. The owner should be able to see the cost and required adapters for a small useful setup before considering a larger installation.</p><ul class="plain-list"><li>Interview a few neighbouring owners about their exact equipment, the reading they wish they had, and their most recent upgrade.</li><li>Try the equipment search with those models. Record missing research, driver work and installation requirements separately.</li><li>Define one viable starter configuration, including the total hardware and installation cost, once validated.</li><li>Track catalogue search → model enquiry → qualified setup. Use actual outcomes before making savings or simplicity claims.</li></ul></section>
<section class="subsection"><span class="micro">Repository evidence / Claim boundaries</span><h2>The numbers need<br />to mean what they say.</h2><p>The current catalogue contains {counts.entries.toLocaleString('en')} model-table entries. {counts.connected} belong to communication or I/O research and {counts.passive} to passive equipment. The communication/I/O groups contain {counts.profiles} protocol profiles.</p><div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Catalogue audit"><table class="source-table"><thead><tr><th>Source group</th><th>Profiles</th><th>Model-table entries</th></tr></thead><tbody>{catalogueGroups.map(g=><tr><th>{g.family}</th><td>{g.profiles}</td><td>{g.entries}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div><p class="small-note">Method: parse Model tables only beneath Dialect headings in the eight catalogue files. Exclude README examples. Count rows without expanding series or inferring unique devices. Profile groupings do not guarantee a model’s electrical transport.</p><ul class="plain-list"><li><strong>Do not say “1,124 supported devices.”</strong> The corpus includes research of varying confidence, passive gear, series and repeated models.</li><li><strong>No shipped support count was established.</strong> The current Rust dialect file has two partial register-map modules; this does not establish complete, hardware-validated model support.</li><li><strong>Open across the stack is the intended direction.</strong> Rust declares MIT OR Apache-2.0, but public source access, complete licence files, hardware licensing and app release scope still need resolution.</li><li><strong>Avoid absolute promises.</strong> Do not claim every brand works, adapters are never needed, or a fixed amount will be saved.</li></ul><a class="text-action" href="/next/open/">Review the open-stack release status ↗</a></section>
<section class="subsection"><span class="micro">Visual direction</span><h2>More product evidence.<br />Less decoration.</h2><p>A uses a light equipment composition and model search. B uses a blue, app-led opening about checking a site before a trip. C uses exposed hardware and ownership. All use the actual Plate 89 assets, Inter Tight and IBM Plex Mono. Buddy appears where assistance is relevant.</p><p style="margin-top:20px">The first-round pages remain available for comparison. The new routes share a complete product, site, equipment, open-stack and enquiry structure. No invented customer installations, testimonials, prices or shipping claims are used.</p></section>
</section></Shell>
